<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 9 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Law and Government</h1><p class="path-tagline">From Hammurabi's stele to the Napoleonic Code: how empires wrote down the rules.</p><p class="path-intro">Conquest wins territory, but only law and administration hold it. Rulers across the world have codified laws, set out principles of statecraft and built institutions to bind diverse subjects to a single authority. This path follows some of the most influential examples: the laws of Hammurabi, the Arthashastra's manual of statecraft, Justinian's codification of Roman law, the Tang Code that shaped East Asia, Mali's founding charter and Napoleon's civil code, whose influence still reaches across continents.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Thinker</a> · <a href="/start">the Builder</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/babylonian-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sin-muballit.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · 1894 BCE – 1595 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Babylonian Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Hammurabi turned a modest kingdom into the master of Mesopotamia through patient preparation, shrewd diplomacy and rapid conquest.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Work · c. 1754 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Code of Hammurabi</span><span class="path-step-why">Inscribed on a basalt stele now in the Louvre, its 282 laws regulated wages, loans, contracts and the duties of merchants.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/arthashastra"><span class="path-step-ph">3</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Work</span><span class="path-step-name">Arthashastra</span><span class="path-step-why">Attributed to Chanakya, this guide to administration, espionage, law and diplomacy is often used to illuminate Mauryan government.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/byzantine-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-arab-siege-of-constantinople.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · 330 CE – 1453 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Byzantine Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Byzantium joined Roman codified law and professional administration to a Christian ideology of the emperor as God's chosen representative.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-juris-civilis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/corpus-juris-civilis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Work · 534 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Corpus Juris Civilis</span><span class="path-step-why">Justinian's codification of 529 to 534, rediscovered and taught at Bologna, became the foundation of civil law across much of the world.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/tang-code"><span class="path-step-ph">6</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Work · 653</span><span class="path-step-name">Tang Code</span><span class="path-step-why">First issued in 624, the Tang Code influenced the legal systems of Japan, Korea and Vietnam, Japan's Taihō Code among them.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/kouroukan-fouga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kouroukan-fouga.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Work</span><span class="path-step-name">Kouroukan Fouga</span><span class="path-step-why">The assembly and charter credited to Sundiata, regulating succession, clan obligations and social order in the Mali Empire.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/french-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Profile · 1534 CE – 1980 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">French Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">France carried its law overseas: in Algeria French civil law applied to European settlers, while most Algerians lived under the indigénat.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Encyclopedia · Work</span><span class="path-step-name">Napoleonic Code</span><span class="path-step-why">Spread across Europe in Napoleon's wake and later by colonial rule, it shaped legal systems in Africa and the Americas.</span></span></a></li></ol>
